DEFECT FOUND (item 3 — comment accuracy, in scope):

handlers_rates.go:84-91, the exact block item (3) was scoped to fix, now
reads:

    // ... reaches prepare.ValidatePersons deep inside the save,
    // which returns a TYPED error (prepare.PersonBirthMonthError /
    // PersonBirthMonthAfterStartError / InvalidStartDateError, each
    // carrying the failing row's ID); statusForMutationError has no
    // case for any of them and falls through to 500.

This claims all three listed types carry "the failing row's ID."
`InvalidStartDateError` does not: `internal/services/retirement/prepare/validate.go:16-18`
defines it as `type InvalidStartDateError struct { Err error }` — one
field, no PersonID, no ID of any kind — and its own doc comment
(validate.go:10-15) says "callers needing to distinguish this from a
per-person failure should use errors.As", i.e. it is explicitly NOT a
per-row error. `grep -rn InvalidStartDateError internal --include='*.go'`
confirms every construction site (`validate.go:106`,
`&InvalidStartDateError{Err: err}`) passes only Err, never an ID.
personsSaveErrorMessage's OWN docstring three lines below (handlers_rates.go:134-137,
unchanged since attempt 5) gets this right: "a failing PERSON only by that
typed error's PersonID" — correctly scoping the ID-bearing claim to the
person case only. The freshly-written block at 88-90 does not carry that
distinction and is factually wrong about InvalidStartDateError.

This is the exact class of error the whole 2026-09-24g/h/i/k chain exists
to eliminate — a comment that misdescribes which typed error carries
identifying data. Item (3) required the stale comment "corrected"; the
delivered text trades one inaccuracy (attempt 5's "plain, untyped error")
for another (this one), so item (3) is not fully satisfied. This is a
comment-only defect with no runtime effect (confirmed by the AST-diff and
mutation work above) — the remedy is a one-line wording fix, not a redo of
items 1-2, which are solid.

No other defect found. Judgment on materiality (comment-only, no
behavioural or test-integrity impact) is left to the lead; reporting as
FAIL per the checker contract ("Is the new comment accurate against the
code?" was explicitly on the attack list and the answer is no, for one of
the three listed types).
